  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 235 AM Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Isolated to scattered showers and storms will \ncontinue each day, mainly afternoon and evening, through the middle \nof next week. Isolated heavy downpours remain possible through Sat, \nmainly over the west, but no widespread severe weather or major \nflooding concerns are expected. Day-to-day convection details will \nremain hard to pinpoint more than 36-48 hours out.\n\nA weak mid level trough will hold just W of the S Appalachians into \nFri, sandwiched between strong anticyclones over the Desert \nSouthwest and off the Mid Atlantic coast. This weakening shear axis \nwill dissipate as it gets absorbed by a broad northern stream trough \n(associated with a large/deep gyre over central Canada) from the \nUpper Midwest into the Ohio Valley. This latter trough will head \neast toward the Appalachians over the weekend but dampen in favor of \nthe longer wave trough just on its heels, with energy diving into \nthe Upper Midwest maintaining broad troughing into the Great Lakes. \nThe overall result will be a somewhat persistent pattern into the \nweekend, with a baggy mid level shear axis holding to our W, keeping \nus in an anticyclonic SSW-SW low-mid level flow, while subtle \nperturbations brush our northern border, and the surface pattern \nstays ill-defined with a weak MSLP gradient and developing weak lee \ntrough over the Piedmont. PW will remain near to just slightly above \nnormal, with poor deep layer bulk shear expected, so despite late-\nday SBCAPE potentially reaching 1500-2500 J/kg, coverage should be a \nbit limited with a lack of notable dynamic contribution, so will max \nout coverage as scattered. The risk for heavy downpours and training \ncells leading to localized mainly urban flooding persists into the \nweekend, primarily in the W where mid level lapse rates should be \nslightly higher than in our E, but don't anticipate any widespread \nflooding problems.\n\nMid level flow becomes even weaker and more westerly late in the \nweekend into early next week, as the westerlies remain to our N and \nthe flattening mid level ridge off the Southeast coast expands \nwestward over FL/AL/GA, helping to curb access to the Gulf and \nAtlantic moisture sources and further driving down our PW. This \nshould result in waning coverage, with a focus on mountain \nconvection shifting into the NW Piedmont. With expected lower cloud \ncover, we'll see warming at the surface as well as aloft, with a lee \ntrough persisting, and the hottest days of this stretch are expected \nto be Mon/Tue, perhaps into Wed, with highs in the low-mid 90s. By \nmid into late next week, as the Canadian gyre digs a bit, the \nwesterlies will dip further S over the Mid Atlantic, and the \nresulting acceleration of increasingly (but broadly) cyclonic mid \nlevel flow overhead may support an uptick in coverage as we approach \nWed/Thu.",
